/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055801 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "26d9f343-f87d-553f-aa68-d391f27f3851" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055801 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055802 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055802 #edb/ref 1061 52734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055802 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "de1acfcd-2302-546a-810d-d29cfbdb68f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055802 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055803 #edb/ref 1061 52737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055803 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "59c52f41-a24c-5ec6-b4b2-9dbee3070bc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055803 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056026 #edb/ref 1064 "Solving a linear equation involves **isolating** the variable on one side of the equation.\n\nThis can often be accomplished using the **addition principle**. The addition principle tells us that adding (or subtracting) the same number on both sides of an equation does not change its solution.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to solve the equation\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 5\n$$\n\nThe variable $x$ is not isolated, because we also have a $+4$ on the left-hand side.\n\nTo isolate $x$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *subtracting* $4$ from $x$. This way, the $+4$ and $-4$ will cancel out to $0$, which will leave $x$ alone. Remember to do this to *both* sides of the equation!\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x + 4 &= 5 \\\\ x+4\\,{\\color{red}{-}}\\,{\\color{red}{4}} &=5\\,{\\color{red}{-}}\\,{\\color{red}{4}} \\\\ x+(4-4) &=(5-4) \\\\ x + 0 &= 1 \\\\ x &= 1. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=1$ is our solution. Let's check that our solution is correct by plugging it back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x + 4 &= 5 \\\\ 1 + 4&= 5 \\\\ 5 &=5 \\;{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}} \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056026 #edb/ref 1065 8126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056026 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1eab91ef-23e4-5e41-9ef4-72aa3c0eb594" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056026 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
